ctions, but also unfriendly, not good at criticism, unorganized. Crew cooperation is a concern. B also has uncooperative and grumpy issues per notes. Actually notes: B can be grumpy, uncooperative. Agent 2 earlier said B can be uncooperative. So B also has crew cooperation concerns. But we have more direct evidence of stress resilience for B. C's stress resilience unknown. Decision quality unknown for both. C's leadership may help decision quality. B's quick decisions not mentioned. A quick decisions but communication issues.\n\nWe need to decide. The panel hasn't covered all criteria yet, but we can still give provisional recommendation. We can either maintain C, argue that despite lack of direct stress evidence, C's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and leadership suggests strong decision quality and situational awareness, and that his cooperation issues (unfriendly) may be less severe than B's uncooperative and pretentious behavior. However B's stress handling is explicit. Which is stronger? The panel may need to decide.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Do not overvalue one impressive strength while ignoring serious concerns on other safety-relevant criteria.\" For C, serious concerns: unfriendly, not good at criticism, unorganized. For B, concerns: pretentious, wrong tone, grumpy, uncooperative. Both have crew cooperation concerns. B's stress resilience is proven; C's unknown. That seems a critical gap. So perhaps we should shift to B.\n\nThus Agent 1 may change vote to Candidate B, citing lack of stress evidence for C and B's proven stress handling, while acknowledging B's communication issues but manageable.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 2's argument, Agent 1's earlier note about lack of stress evidence. Provide explicit facts: B handles stress very well, very conscientious; C can anticipate dangerous situations but no stress evidence; C unfriendly; B pretentious but can be coached.\n\nThus produce public message: I now recommend B, expla